Verification Reference

Verification Objective

Demonstrate that the Adaptive Vehicle Intelligence Architecture (AVIA) can execute approved decisions as intended, within applicable execution constraints, and provide reliable execution-state information.

---

Verification Methods

Verification shall be achieved through one or more of the following methods:

Method| Purpose
Analysis| Confirm that the execution architecture can translate approved decisions into defined executable actions within applicable constraints.
Demonstration| Demonstrate representative approved decisions being converted into corresponding vehicle actions.
Test| Evaluate execution behavior under defined operating conditions, including successful, partial, delayed, and failed execution scenarios.
Inspection| Confirm that required execution interfaces, state reporting, and traceability mechanisms are present and correctly implemented.

---

Verification Success Criteria

Verification shall demonstrate that SO-F-005 can:

- Receive an approved decision through the defined execution interface.
- Translate the approved decision into the corresponding executable action.
- Execute the action within applicable engineering and operational constraints.
- Detect relevant execution deviations.
- Detect relevant execution failures.
- Produce appropriate execution-state information.
- Preserve traceability between the approved decision, resulting action, and execution state.
- Report execution outcomes through the defined interfaces.

---

Execution Verification Boundary

Verification shall evaluate the execution capability independently from:

- Perception.
- Contextual interpretation.
- Decision generation.
- Decision authorization.

The verification shall establish that SO-F-005 correctly executes an approved input.

It shall not establish that the decision was correctly authorized.

---

Authorization Verification Boundary

Authorization shall be verified through the authority architecture once that architecture has been established.

The current verification structure shall therefore preserve the distinction:

Generated Decision
       ↓
[Authorization Verification — Future]
       ↓
Approved Decision
       ↓
[SO-F-005 Verification]
       ↓
Executed Action

Evidence from SO-F-005 shall not be used as evidence that authorization occurred.

---

Execution Failure Verification

Verification shall include appropriate failure scenarios to establish that SO-F-005 can:

- Detect inability to execute an approved action.
- Detect significant deviation from the intended action.
- Represent the resulting execution state.
- Report the condition through the appropriate interface.

Verification shall not require SO-F-005 to independently determine the subsequent adaptive response unless that responsibility is later established through an approved architectural decision.

---

Execution-State Verification

Where execution-state information is required, verification shall establish that the reported state accurately represents the relevant execution condition.

Potential state categories may include:

- Executing.
- Completed.
- Partially completed.
- Delayed.
- Deviating.
- Failed.

These categories remain subject to refinement through system-specific requirements.

---

Traceability Verification

Verification shall demonstrate the ability to establish the relationship:

Approved Decision
       ↓
Executed Action
       ↓
Execution State

Where the architecture provides the necessary upstream information, this lineage may extend to:

Operating Context
       ↓
Generated Decision
       ↓
Authorization Evidence
       ↓
Approved Decision
       ↓
Executed Action
       ↓
Execution State

